  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through Tuesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: A storm system will move eastward into the \nRocky Mountains today. Low temperatures throughout Northern and \nCentral Nevada tonight will be around five degrees below normal for \nthis time of year. Minimum apparent temperatures in the coldest \nvalleys of Northern and Central Nevada tonight will be in the teens. \nA ridge of high pressure will build eastward across Southern Idaho \nThursday and Friday, leading to a warming trend. High temperatures \nacross Northern and Central Nevada Thursday afternoon will be about \nfive degrees warmer than this afternoon. Maximum temperatures \nthroughout Northern and Central Nevada Friday afternoon will be a \ncouple of degrees above seasonal values. A pleasant and dry weekend \nis in store for the Silver State. \n\nLooking at our next set-up, those of us who greatly enjoy winter \nweather will be very pleased as a polar jet has the potential of \nworking it\u2019s way back into our area. This will contain a deep low \nbringing a strong cold front through our area early next week, \ndropping highs back down to the 40s and 50s, and our lows into the \n20s and 30s. This front will have the potential to bring \nprecipitation next Monday and Tuesday, but confidence in timing and \namounts are low as we are nearly a full week away.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence in dry and warming \nconditions starting Thursday. Moderate confidence in temperatures \ndecreasing along the front next week. Low confidence in \nprecipitation types, placement, and timing next week. No deviations \nfrom the NBM model output.",
